## Summary

I delivered a production-quality native **Apple `AnomalyDetectorWidget`** dashboard surface at parity with `features/dashboard/widgets/AnomalyDetectorWidget.tsx`, committed as `d3364134`.

**What shipped** (`apps/apple/Sources/Dashboard/Widgets/`, split into ≤400-line files per the repo's swiftlint convention):
- `AnomalyDetectorData.swift` — SI-faithful domain model (`AnomalySeverity`/`Impact`/`Entry`/`Report` with snake_case `Codable`), web-parity relative-time bucketing, and the registry descriptor (`anomaly-detector`, 2×4 default / 1×2 min / 4×40 max with clamping).
- `AnomalyDetectorFeed.swift` — the P1/S8 `LoadableState`-based data port (`AnomalyFeeding` + static/mutable feeds) and the `view.opened` telemetry seam. No HTTP in the view.
- `AnomalyDetectorModel.swift` — `@Observable` view model deriving every state, sorted tips, severity, freshness.
- `AnomalyDetectorChrome.swift` / `AnomalyDetectorWidget.swift` — SwiftUI chrome (skeleton/retry/freshness chip) + compact & full layouts rendering **all six states** (loading, empty, error, stale, offline, content), with VoiceOver labels and previews.
- `AnomalyDetectorWidgetTests.swift` — 20 XCTest cases (JSON→projection adapter, per-state derivation, relative-time/severity parity, telemetry, a11y, registry, feed binding).
- 19 `widget.anomalyDetector.*` keys added to `Localizable.xcstrings` (+ mirrored in the `web/src/i18n/en.json` source).

**Verification (gate log: `.github/prompts/monorepo/logs/p4-apple-dashboard-widgets-0003-AnomalyDetectorWidget.log`):**
- `swiftlint --strict` → **0 violations / 262 files**; `swiftformat --lint` → **0/266**; `check-placeholders` → **0** (EXIT=0).
- iOS **TEST BUILD SUCCEEDED** and macOS **BUILD SUCCEEDED** (Swift 6 strict concurrency).
- iOS `TeslaSyncTests` → **275 tests, 0 failures** (the 20 new tests + no regressions). A test bug I introduced (premature "no data" phase) was caught by the suite and fixed.

The iOS UI-test and macOS test-execution steps of the full `build test` are environment-blocked (headless CoreSimulator has no accessibility server; no macOS signing certificate; a pre-existing `TEST_HOST` product-name mismatch) and are documented with evidence in the log — none are caused by this surface. The pre-existing admin-file edits in the working tree were left untouched.

